--- MESSAGE ---
The JSON corpus is re-canonicalized from the drake, and the host-opaque call strings become trees

Every definition with a .drake now carries the JSON that Drake.parse reads from it: 40 files rewritten
through DrakeCLI parse, 62 already identical. The ~105 host-opaque strings the carrier held for calls
are the trees the call syntax reads from the surface, and the JSON says exactly what the drake says.

Three definitions are left as they were, each for a stated reason: BodyElement and ActorAspect, whose
authored-ahead drake carries aspects their JSON deliberately lacks; and format/json/Value, whose drake
carries lambdas and if/then/else in Haskell form that the parser still returns as opaque text (GitHub
#61) — the one place the JSON still knows more than parse reads. The 38 mods definitions with no .drake
have nothing to canonicalize from.

One Scala change, three lines: two factory defaults go from Seq() to Seq.empty, because the drake
spells an empty default [] and that is the tree it parses to — the empty-collection spelling loss
closing, with the Scala following the JSON. Verified before handover against the committed Scala,
per type and for the TypeElement family, with the compiled engine; no test changes.

